free_fns.doc:
The process-wide seed every hashed container starts from, drawn once from
 the platform's entropy on first use. A program that builds no hash table
 draws nothing.

 A per-process seed makes the bucket layout, and with it the iteration order,
 differ between runs, so a key set chosen to collide in one run does not
 collide in the next. Pass `hash_seed=0`, or any fixed value, when a run has
 to be reproducible.

 ```mbt check
 test {
   let a = @spec.hash_seed()
   let b = @spec.hash_seed()
   @test.assert_eq(a, b)
 }
 ```

A seed derived from bytes the caller already holds, which is how a service
 reproduces the layout of its tables across restarts: pass the same bytes and
 every container lays out the same way.

 ```mbt check
 test {
   @test.assert_eq(
     @spec.hash_seed_from(b"moonstl"),
     @spec.hash_seed_from(b"moonstl"),
   )
   @test.assert_not_eq(
     @spec.hash_seed_from(b"moonstl"),
     @spec.hash_seed_from(b"moonst"),
   )
 }
 ```

Whether this platform gave real entropy to `hash_seed`. `false` means the
 platform has no source — a wasm host without WASI — and every hashed
 container runs from a seed of zero, which is still correct, only
 predictable.

 ```mbt check
 test {
   let _ = @spec.hash_seed()
   let random = @spec.hash_seed_is_random()
   @test.assert_eq(random, @spec.hash_seed_is_random())
 }
 ```

types.doc:
A set of non-negative integers stored as bits: membership is a bit test, and
 the set operations run word by word. `length` counts the elements, not the
 range, because the range has no ceiling.

 ```mbt check
 test {
   let set : @seq.BitSet = @seq.BitSet::new()
   @test.assert_eq(set.insert(3), true)
   @test.assert_eq(set.insert(3), false)
   @test.assert_eq(set.contains(3), true)
   @test.assert_eq(set.length(), 1)
   @test.assert_eq(set.select(0), Some(3))
   @test.assert_eq(set.rank(4), 1)
 }
 ```

A vector of bits with a length: bit `i` lives in word `i / 64` at bit
 `i % 64`, and the bits past the length are always zero.

 ```mbt check
 test {
   let bits : @seq.BitVec = @seq.BitVec::new()
   bits.push(true)
   bits.push(false)
   bits.push(true)
   @test.assert_eq(bits.length(), 3)
   @test.assert_eq(bits.get(2), Some(true))
   @test.assert_eq(bits.count_ones(), 2)
 }
 ```

An array with a hole in it: the elements before the hole are kept in order,
 the ones after it are kept reversed, and moving the hole is the only
 operation that shifts more than one element. Typing at the hole is therefore
 constant time, which is what a text editor's buffer wants.

 ```mbt check
 test {
   let buffer : @seq.GapBuffer[Int32] = @seq.GapBuffer::new()
   buffer.insert(0, 1)
   buffer.insert(1, 3)
   buffer.insert(1, 2)
   @test.assert_eq(buffer.to_array(), [1, 2, 3])
   @test.assert_eq(buffer.remove(1), Some(2))
   @test.assert_eq(buffer.to_array(), [1, 3])
 }
 ```

A doubly linked list: splicing one list onto another and splitting a list in
 two are constant time, because neither moves an element — they only relink
 nodes. Indexing walks, which is the price of that.

 ```mbt check
 test {
   let list : @seq.LinkedList[Int32] = @seq.LinkedList::new()
   list.push_back(1)
   list.push_back(2)
   list.push_front(0)
   @test.assert_eq(list.to_array(), [0, 1, 2])
   @test.assert_eq(list.pop_front(), Some(0))
   @test.assert_eq(list.length(), 2)
 }
 ```

members.doc:
An empty buffer with a ceiling of `capacity` elements. A ceiling of zero or
 less is refused because a buffer that can hold nothing could never accept a
 push.

 ```mbt check
 test {
   let buffer : @seq.RingBuffer[Int32] = @seq.RingBuffer::new(capacity=2)
   @test.assert_eq(buffer.capacity(), 2)
   assert_true(buffer.is_empty())
 }
 ```

types.doc:
A set whose elements are kept densely, one array for the elements and one
 index from element to its position. Membership is a lookup and iteration
 walks contiguous memory, which is what a caller who filters a dense
 collection wants; the price is a hash index beside the array.

 ```mbt check
 test {
   let set : @seq.SparseSet[String] = @seq.SparseSet::new()
   @test.assert_eq(set.insert("a"), true)
   @test.assert_eq(set.insert("a"), false)
   @test.assert_eq(set.contains("a"), true)
   @test.assert_eq(set.to_array(), ["a"])
 }
 ```

A two-way map: the pair `k -> v` can be read from either side, and neither a
 key nor a value can appear twice. `set` keeps the mapping total by dropping
 whatever the new pair collides with, and hands it back.

 ```mbt check
 test {
   let bimap : @map.BiMap[Int32, String] = @map.BiMap::new()
   let _ = bimap.set(1, "a")
   let _ = bimap.set(2, "b")
   @test.assert_eq(bimap.get_key("b"), Some(2))
   @test.assert_eq(bimap.set(1, "b"), Some((1, "a")))
   @test.assert_eq(bimap.get(1), Some("b"))
   @test.assert_eq(bimap.get(2), None)
 }
 ```

A stack of maps read as one: a lookup falls through the stack from the front,
 and a write always lands in the front map. That is what a layered
 configuration needs — a child scope that sees its parent's settings but
 shadows them without touching them.

 ```mbt check
 test {
   let base : Map[String, Int32] = Map([])
   base.set("a", 1)
   let chain : @map.ChainMap[String, Int32] = @map.ChainMap::new([base])
   @test.assert_eq(chain.get("a"), Some(1))
   let child = chain.new_child()
   child.set("a", 2)
   @test.assert_eq(child.get("a"), Some(2))
   @test.assert_eq(chain.get("a"), Some(1))
 }
 ```

A multiset of counts: each key carries how many times it was seen, and a key
 whose count reaches zero leaves the counter so that iteration only ever sees
 keys that are present.

 ```mbt check
 test {
   let counter : @map.Counter[String] = @map.Counter::new()
   @test.assert_eq(counter.incr("a"), 1)
   @test.assert_eq(counter.incr_by("a", 2), 3)
   @test.assert_eq(counter.decr("a"), 2)
   @test.assert_eq(counter.total(), 2L)
 }
 ```

A map that answers a lookup with a value it makes up on the spot: the
 factory is called with the key, so a reader never has to write the
 check-then-insert pair by hand.

 ```mbt check
 test {
   let map : @map.DefaultMap[String, Int32] = @map.DefaultMap::new(factory=key => {
     key.length()
   })
   @test.assert_eq(map.get_or_init("abc"), 3)
   @test.assert_eq(map.length(), 1)
   @test.assert_eq(map.get("abc"), Some(3))
 }
 ```

A map held as a sorted array of keys with a parallel array of values: a
 lookup is a binary search over contiguous memory, which is the shape to
 reach for when a table is built once and read many times.

 ```mbt check
 test {
   let map : @map.FlatMap[Int32, String] = @map.FlatMap::new()
   let _ = map.set(2, "b")
   let _ = map.set(1, "a")
   @test.assert_eq(map.keys().to_array(), [1, 2])
   @test.assert_eq(map.get(2), Some("b"))
   @test.assert_eq(map.index_of(3), Err(2))
 }
 ```

A multimap held as a sorted array of pairs: keys in order, and the values of
 one key in order among themselves. Repeats are kept, so this is the flat
 counterpart of `SortedMultiMap`.

 ```mbt check
 test {
   let map : @map.FlatMultiMap[Int32, String] = @map.FlatMultiMap::new()
   map.insert(2, "b")
   map.insert(1, "a")
   map.insert(1, "c")
   @test.assert_eq(map.get(1), ["a", "c"])
   @test.assert_eq(map.length(), 3)
   @test.assert_eq(map.key_count(), 2)
 }
 ```

A set held as a sorted array of elements, the companion of `FlatMap`.

 ```mbt check
 test {
   let set : @map.FlatSet[Int32] = @map.FlatSet::new()
   @test.assert_eq(set.insert(3), true)
   @test.assert_eq(set.insert(3), false)
   @test.assert_eq(set.get_index(0), Some(3))
 }
 ```

An associative container that keeps insertion order and gives every entry a
 position: `get_index` reads by position, `get_index_of` reads the position of
 a key, and the entries can be reordered without touching the table's
 contents. `swap_remove` is the default removal because it is O(1); a caller
 that needs the remaining positions to stay contiguous uses `shift_remove`.

 ```mbt check
 test {
   let map : @map.IndexMap[String, Int32] = @map.IndexMap::new()
   let _ = map.set("a", 1)
   let _ = map.set("b", 2)
   @test.assert_eq(map.get_index_of("b"), Some(1))
   @test.assert_eq(map.get_index(0), Some(("a", 1)))
   let _ = map.swap_remove("a")
   @test.assert_eq(map.get_index(0), Some(("b", 2)))
 }
 ```

A set that keeps insertion order and gives every element a position, the
 companion of `IndexMap` the way `HashSet` is the companion of `HashMap`.

 ```mbt check
 test {
   let set : @map.IndexSet[Int32] = @map.IndexSet::new()
   @test.assert_eq(set.insert(7), (0, true))
   @test.assert_eq(set.insert(7), (0, false))
   @test.assert_eq(set.get_index(0), Some(7))
 }
 ```

A map from a key to a list of values: one entry per key, and the values of a
 key keep the order they were inserted in.

 ```mbt check
 test {
   let map : @map.MultiMap[String, Int32] = @map.MultiMap::new()
   map.insert("a", 1)
   map.insert("a", 2)
   @test.assert_eq(map.get("a"), [1, 2])
   @test.assert_eq(map.length(), 2)
   @test.assert_eq(map.key_count(), 1)
 }
 ```

A trie with its chains of single-child nodes collapsed into one edge per
 label: a lookup compares whole labels instead of single characters, which is
 what keeps a tree over long shared prefixes shallow. The surface is the
 `Trie` surface, plus the queries that only make sense over a compressed tree:
 the keys that are prefixes of a given key, a count of the keys under a
 prefix, and a scan of that subtree.

 ```mbt check
 test {
   let tree : @map.RadixTree[Int32] = @map.RadixTree::new()
   @test.assert_eq(tree.insert("car", 1), None)
   @test.assert_eq(tree.insert("cart", 2), None)
   @test.assert_eq(tree.insert("cat", 3), None)
   @test.assert_eq(tree.get("cart"), Some(2))
   @test.assert_eq(tree.get_ancestor_keys("cartoon"), ["car", "cart"])
   @test.assert_eq(tree.prefix_count("ca"), 3)
 }
 ```

A value that holds over a span of keys: the ranges are kept sorted, never
 overlap each other, and two ranges that touch and hold the same value are
 kept as one. A range is half-open — it covers `start` and stops before `end`
 — so an insert that meets a neighbour at one endpoint does not collide with
 it.

 ```mbt check
 test {
   let map : @map.RangeMap[Int32, String] = @map.RangeMap::new()
   map.insert(start=0, end=10, "low")
   map.insert(start=10, end=20, "high")
   @test.assert_eq(map.get(5), Some("low"))
   @test.assert_eq(map.get(10), Some("high"))
   @test.assert_eq(map.get(20), None)
   @test.assert_eq(map.length(), 2)
 }
 ```

A set of spans: the companion of `RangeMap` with no value to carry.

 ```mbt check
 test {
   let set : @map.RangeSet[Int32] = @map.RangeSet::new()
   set.insert(start=0, end=5)
   set.insert(start=5, end=9)
   @test.assert_eq(set.contains(7), true)
   @test.assert_eq(set.contains(9), false)
   @test.assert_eq(set.length(), 1)
 }
 ```

A multimap held as a sorted array: the keys are in order, and the values of
 one key are in order among themselves as well, so `range` and `first` walk
 the pairs in the order a reader expects.

 ```mbt check
 test {
   let map : @map.SortedMultiMap[Int32, String] = @map.SortedMultiMap::new()
   map.insert(2, "b")
   map.insert(1, "a")
   map.insert(1, "b")
   @test.assert_eq(map.first(), Some((1, "a")))
   @test.assert_eq(map.get(1), ["a", "b"])
 }
 ```

A map keyed by `String` that shares the storage of keys with a common
 prefix: the walk to a key costs one step per character, whatever else is
 stored.

 ```mbt check
 test {
   let trie : @map.Trie[Int32] = @map.Trie::new()
   @test.assert_eq(trie.insert("car", 1), None)
   @test.assert_eq(trie.insert("cart", 2), None)
   @test.assert_eq(trie.get("car"), Some(1))
   @test.assert_eq(trie.keys_with_prefix("ca"), ["car", "cart"])
   @test.assert_eq(trie.longest_prefix("cartoon"), Some(("cart", 2)))
 }
 ```

members.doc:
Builds a heap from `items` in O(n), by sifting down from the last parent
 rather than pushing one at a time.

 ```mbt check
 test {
   let heap : @heap.DaryHeap[Int32] = try! @heap.DaryHeap::from_array([
     5, 1, 4, 1, 3,
   ])
   assert_true(heap.into_sorted_array() == [1, 1, 3, 4, 5])
 }
 ```

Drains the heap into an array in pop order, leaving it empty.

 ```mbt check
 test {
   let heap : @heap.DaryHeap[Int32] = try! @heap.DaryHeap::from_array([3, 1, 2])
   assert_true(heap.into_sorted_array() == [1, 2, 3])
   assert_true(heap.is_empty())
 }
 ```

Builds a min or max heap ordered by `T : Compare`.

 `arity` is the number of children a node has and has to be at least 2;
 anything smaller is refused because it does not describe a tree.

Adds one element in O(log_d n).

 ```mbt check
 test {
   let heap : @heap.DaryHeap[Int32] = try! @heap.DaryHeap::new()
   heap.push(2)
   heap.push(1)
   assert_true(heap.peek() == Some(1))
 }
 ```

types.doc:
A Fibonacci heap: roots and children sit in circular doubly linked lists,
 so an insert only splices one node in, a meld only joins two lists, and a
 `decrease_key` only cuts one subtree loose — each of them constant amortised
 time. The price is paid on `pop`, which consolidates the roots by degree.

 ```mbt check
 test {
   let heap : @heap.FibonacciHeap[Int32] = @heap.FibonacciHeap::new()
   let a = heap.insert(5)
   let _ = heap.insert(2)
   let _ = heap.insert(8)
   assert_true(heap.peek() == Some(2))
   assert_true(heap.decrease_key(a, 1))
   assert_true(heap.pop() == Some(1))
 }
 ```

A binary heap whose entries are addressed by `HeapKey`: an `update` or a
 `remove_at` reaches one element without searching, because every slot knows
 its position in the heap array and every position knows its slot.

 ```mbt check
 test {
   let heap : @heap.IndexHeap[Int32] = @heap.IndexHeap::new()
   let a = heap.insert(5)
   let b = heap.insert(1)
   assert_true(heap.update(a, 0))
   assert_true(heap.peek() == Some(0))
   assert_true(heap.remove_at(b) == Some(1))
   assert_true(heap.length() == 1)
 }
 ```

A double-ended priority queue: both the smallest and the largest element are
 one comparison away, and both can be popped in logarithmic time. The array
 alternates min levels and max levels, so the smallest is at the root and the
 largest is one of its two children.

 ```mbt check
 test {
   let heap : @heap.MinMaxHeap[Int32] = @heap.MinMaxHeap::new()
   for value in [5, 1, 9, 3, 7] {
     heap.push(value)
   }
   assert_true(heap.peek_min() == Some(1))
   assert_true(heap.peek_max() == Some(9))
   assert_true(heap.pop_min() == Some(1))
   assert_true(heap.pop_max() == Some(9))
 }
 ```

A heap of linked nodes: every node keeps its children in a list and a meld
 only links two roots, so `insert` and `meld` are constant time and a pop pays
 for the pairwise merging of the children it uncovers. Its `update` and
 `remove_at` reach a node through a `HeapKey` and detach it without a search,
 which is what a scheduler that cancels timers needs.

 ```mbt check
 test {
   let heap : @heap.PairingHeap[Int32] = @heap.PairingHeap::new()
   let a = heap.insert(5)
   let b = heap.insert(2)
   let _ = heap.insert(9)
   assert_true(heap.peek() == Some(2))
   assert_true(heap.update(a, 1))
   assert_true(heap.peek() == Some(1))
   assert_true(heap.remove_at(b) == Some(2))
   assert_true(heap.pop() == Some(1))
 }
 ```

A tree whose nodes live in one arena and are reached by id: a node knows its
 parent, its first and last child, and its two siblings, so every step of a
 walk is a field read and no pointer chases the allocator. Children are kept
 in insertion order, and `sort_children` reorders them by their own values.

 ```mbt check
 test {
   let tree : @tree.ArenaTree[String] = @tree.ArenaTree::new()
   let root = tree.add("root")
   let a = tree.add_child(root, "a")
   let _ = tree.add_child(root, "b")
   let _ = tree.add_child(a, "a1")
   assert_true(tree.length() == 4)
   assert_true(tree.children(root).length() == 2)
   assert_true(tree.descendants(root).to_array().length() == 3)
   assert_true(tree.remove(a) == Some("a"))
   assert_true(tree.length() == 2)
 }
 ```

Union-find over `0 ..< n`: every set is a tree whose root carries its size,
 and `find` halves the path on the way up so repeated queries flatten the
 structure without a second pass. Union by size keeps the trees shallow, which
 together with path halving makes the amortised cost of an operation inverse
 Ackermann — effectively constant.

 ```mbt check
 test {
   let sets : @tree.DisjointSet = @tree.DisjointSet::new()
   let a = sets.add()
   let b = sets.add()
   let c = sets.add()
   assert_true(sets.union(a, b))
   assert_true(sets.connected(a, b))
   assert_true(!sets.connected(a, c))
   assert_true(sets.set_count() == 2)
 }
 ```

Union-find over keys of any type: the key is interned to an index on first
 use, and the union-find runs on the indices.

 ```mbt check
 test {
   let sets : @tree.DisjointSetMap[String] = @tree.DisjointSetMap::new()
   assert_true(sets.union("a", "b"))
   assert_true(sets.connected("a", "b"))
   assert_true(!sets.connected("a", "c"))
 }
 ```

Intervals over a key type, kept sorted by start with a running maximum of the
 ends. That prefix maximum is what a point query walks back through: once a
 prefix can no longer reach the point, no interval before it can either, so
 the scan stops. Intervals are half-open, `[start, end)`.

 ```mbt check
 test {
   let tree : @tree.IntervalTree[Int32, String] = @tree.IntervalTree::new()
   let _ = tree.insert(start=0, end=10, "a")
   let _ = tree.insert(start=5, end=15, "b")
   assert_true(tree.query_point(7).length() == 2)
   assert_true(tree.query_point(12).length() == 1)
   assert_true(tree.query_point(20).length() == 0)
 }
 ```

A search tree that also answers by position: every node carries the size of
 its subtree, so `select` and `rank` walk one path instead of counting.
 Duplicates are not stored. The shape is that of a treap, which is what keeps
 the depth logarithmic without rebalancing after every insert.

 ```mbt check
 test {
   let tree : @tree.OrderStatisticTree[Int32] = @tree.OrderStatisticTree::new()
   for value in [5, 1, 9, 3] {
     let _ = tree.insert(value)
   }
   assert_true(tree.select(0) == Some(1))
   assert_true(tree.select(3) == Some(9))
   assert_true(tree.rank(5) == 2)
   assert_true(tree.count_range(2, 9) == 2)
 }
 ```

A probabilistic ordered set. A node takes part in one level with probability
 `p` and in the next with `p` again, so a search skips most of the list at the
 top and refines as it descends; every level also stores how many nodes it
 spans, which is what makes `rank` and `select` a single descent. The shape is
 drawn from the seed, so a run replays exactly.

 ```mbt check
 test {
   let list : @tree.SkipList[Int32] = @tree.SkipList::new()
   for value in [5, 1, 9, 3] {
     let _ = list.insert(value)
   }
   assert_true(list.to_array() == [1, 3, 5, 9])
   assert_true(list.rank(5) == 2)
   assert_true(list.select(2) == Some(5))
 }
 ```

A map held as a treap: the keys are kept in order, each node carries a random
 priority that keeps the tree balanced, and `split` and `merge` cut and join
 in one path. `range` walks the keys in order between two bounds.

 ```mbt check
 test {
   let tree : @tree.Treap[Int32, String] = @tree.Treap::new()
   let _ = tree.set(2, "b")
   let _ = tree.set(1, "a")
   let _ = tree.set(3, "c")
   assert_true(tree.get(2) == Some("b"))
   assert_true(tree.to_array() == [(1, "a"), (2, "b"), (3, "c")])
   assert_true(tree.remove(2) == Some("b"))
 }
 ```

A cache that keeps the recency and the frequency of what it sees in balance,
 in the manner of ARC: a hit on a re-requested key raises the share of the
 cache given to frequent entries (`recent_target` moves up), a key that
 falls back into the ghost lists takes that share down. `lists` reports the
 four list sizes, which is how the adaptation is observed.

 ```mbt check
 test {
   let cache : @cache.ArcCache[String, Int32] = @cache.ArcCache::new(capacity=2)
   let _ = cache.set("a", 1)
   let _ = cache.set("b", 2)
   let _ = cache.get("a")
   let _ = cache.set("c", 3)
   let (t1, t2, b1, b2) = cache.lists()
   @test.assert_eq(t1 + t2, 2)
   @test.assert_eq(b1 + b2 <= 2, true)
 }
 ```

members.doc:
The hit rate, or `0` when nothing has been looked up.

 ```mbt check
 test {
   let stats : @cache.CacheStats = { hits: 3, misses: 1, evictions: 0, }
   @test.assert_eq(stats.hit_rate(), 0.75)
 }
 ```

types.doc:
A cache whose entries expire: an entry is dropped when it is read after its
 deadline, when a purge finds it, and by the least-recently-used rule when
 the capacity is full. Expiry is lazy, so a purge is the only thing that
 walks the expired entries in bulk.

 The cache never reads a clock of its own — `now` is the caller's, which is
 what lets a test move time without waiting. `ttl` and every deadline are in
 the units of that clock.

 ```mbt check
 test {
   let mut clock : Int64 = 0L
   let cache : @cache.ExpiringCache[String, Int32] = @cache.ExpiringCache::new(
     ttl=10L,
     capacity=2,
     now=() => clock,
   )
   let _ = cache.set("a", 1)
   @test.assert_eq(cache.get("a"), Some(1))
   clock = 11L
   @test.assert_eq(cache.get("a"), None)
 }
 ```

A least-frequently-used cache: an insert past the capacity drops the entry
 with the smallest access count, and among equals the one that has gone
 untouched the longest. A hit moves the entry into the list of the next
 frequency, which is what keeps both operations O(1).

 ```mbt check
 test {
   let cache : @cache.LfuCache[String, Int32] = @cache.LfuCache::new(capacity=2)
   let _ = cache.set("a", 1)
   let _ = cache.set("b", 2)
   let _ = cache.get("a")
   let _ = cache.set("c", 3)
   @test.assert_eq(cache.get("b"), None)
   @test.assert_eq(cache.frequency("a"), Some(2))
 }
 ```

members.doc:
The value stored under `key`, marking the entry as the most recently used.

 ```mbt check
 test {
   let cache : @cache.LruCache[Int32, String] = @cache.LruCache::new(capacity=1)
   let _ = cache.set(1, "one")
   @test.assert_eq(cache.get(1), Some("one"))
   @test.assert_eq(cache.get(2), None)
   @test.assert_eq(cache.stats().misses, 1L)
 }
 ```

Stores `value` under `key` and returns the entry the insert displaced: the
 pair it replaced when the key was already present, the pair the policy
 dropped otherwise, `None` when there was room. Compare the key of the
 result to tell the two apart.

 ```mbt check
 test {
   let cache : @cache.LruCache[String, Int32] = @cache.LruCache::new(capacity=1)
   @test.assert_eq(cache.set("a", 1), None)
   @test.assert_eq(cache.set("b", 2), Some(("a", 1)))
   @test.assert_eq(cache.set("b", 3), Some(("b", 2)))
 }
 ```

types.doc:
A least-recently-used cache with a fixed capacity: `get` marks an entry as
 the most recent, and an insert past the capacity drops the entry that has
 gone untouched the longest.

 ```mbt check
 test {
   let cache : @cache.LruCache[String, Int32] = @cache.LruCache::new(capacity=2)
   let _ = cache.set("a", 1)
   let _ = cache.set("b", 2)
   let _ = cache.get("a")
   let _ = cache.set("c", 3)
   @test.assert_eq(cache.get("b"), None)
   @test.assert_eq(cache.get("a"), Some(1))
 }
 ```

free_fns.doc:
Wraps `f` so that each argument is computed once and remembered, the way
 `functools.lru_cache` does it: the returned function keeps the last
 `capacity` results and recomputes anything that fell out.

 ```mbt check
 test {
   let mut calls = 0
   let square : (Int32) -> Int32 = @cache.memoize(
     key => {
       calls += 1
       key * key
     },
     capacity=8,
   )
   @test.assert_eq(square(4), 16)
   @test.assert_eq(square(4), 16)
   @test.assert_eq(calls, 1)
 }
 ```

members.doc:
Builds a filter sized for `expected_items` at
 `bloom_options.false_positive_rate`.

 The bit count is `m = -k n / @math.ln(1 - p^(1/k))` with `k = log2(1/p)`, the
 standard optimum; with the default rate of 1% that is 9.6 bits per item and
 7 probes. A `Scalable` filter starts with one layer and adds more as it
 fills.

 ```mbt check
 test {
   let filter : @sketch.BloomFilter[String] = @sketch.BloomFilter::new(
     expected_items=1000,
   ) catch {
     _ => abort("valid parameters")
   }
   assert_false(filter.may_contain("absent"))
   assert_true(filter.insert("present"))
   assert_true(filter.may_contain("present"))
 }
 ```

types.doc:
A sketch that counts: the estimate for an item is the smallest counter its
 probes land on, which never underestimates because every counter it reads
 was incremented by that item too. The width and depth come from the error
 the caller tolerates, so nobody picks them by hand.

 ```mbt check
 test {
   let sketch : @sketch.CountMinSketch[String] = @sketch.CountMinSketch::new(
     epsilon=0.01,
     confidence=0.99,
   )
   for _ in 0..<5 {
     sketch.insert("a")
   }
   assert_true(sketch.estimate("a") >= 5)
   assert_true(sketch.estimate("b") == 0)
   assert_true(sketch.total() == 5L)
 }
 ```

An estimate of how many distinct items were added, from one small register
 per bucket: each item sets its bucket to the longest run of leading zeroes
 its hash produced, and the harmonic mean of the registers is the estimate.
 The error is about `1.04 / sqrt(m)`, so a thousand registers buy three
 percent, and neither the memory nor the insert cost depends on the count.

 ```mbt check
 test {
   let log : @sketch.HyperLogLog[Int32] = @sketch.HyperLogLog::new(precision=12)
   for i in 0..<1000 {
     log.add(i)
   }
   let estimate = log.estimate()
   assert_true(estimate > 900.0)
   assert_true(estimate < 1100.0)
 }
 ```

A digest of a distribution: the values are grouped into centroids, each a
 mean with a weight, and the groups are kept small where the quantiles are
 dense — near the ends — so a quantile costs the same whether the digest has
 seen a thousand values or a million. The compression parameter is how many
 groups the digest is willing to keep; a hundred is the usual choice for a
 relative error of about one percent at the tails.

 ```mbt check
 test {
   let digest : @sketch.TDigest = @sketch.TDigest::new()
   for i in 0..<1000 {
     digest.add(i.to_double())
   }
   let median = digest.quantile(0.5)
   assert_true(median > 450.0)
   assert_true(median < 550.0)
   assert_true(digest.count() == 1000.0)
 }
 ```

A sketch that keeps the `k` smallest hashes it has seen and reports a count
 from the largest one it kept: if the threshold sits at `theta`, then `k / theta`
 scales a sample of `k` hashes to the whole set. Because the kept hashes are
 exactly the ones below the threshold, the estimate is a maximum-likelihood
 one, and two sketches combine by keeping the union's `k` smallest.

 ```mbt check
 test {
   let sketch : @sketch.ThetaSketch[Int32] = @sketch.ThetaSketch::new(
     nominal_entries=1024,
   )
   for i in 0..<500 {
     sketch.add(i)
   }
   assert_true(sketch.estimate() > 450.0)
   assert_true(sketch.estimate() < 550.0)
 }
 ```

One pattern matched with the Knuth-Morris-Pratt scan: O(n + m) time with a
 worst-case guarantee, because the failure table never lets the text pointer
 move backwards. Offsets are UTF-16 code units, the indices `String` uses.

 ```mbt check
 test {
   let kmp = @text.Kmp::new("ab")
   assert_eq(kmp.search("cababc"), Some(1))
   assert_eq(kmp.search_all("abab").to_array(), [0, 2])
 }
 ```

Builds a rope by appending, which is what a reader or a parser wants: one
 chunk per append, and one reindex at the end.

 ```mbt check
 test {
   let builder : @text.RopeBuilder = @text.RopeBuilder::new()
   builder.append("one ")
   builder.append("two")
   let rope = builder.finish()
   assert_true(rope.to_string() == "one two")
 }
 ```

A window into a rope. It holds its own copy of the rope's chunk list, so an
 edit to the rope does not move the text under the window.

 ```mbt check
 test {
   let rope : @text.Rope = @text.Rope::from_string("abcdef")
   let window = rope.slice(start=2, end=4)
   assert_true(window.to_string() == "cd")
   assert_true(window.length() == 2)
 }
 ```

A matcher over two runs: it indexes the second one, finds the longest block
 the two share, and reports the matching blocks and the steps between them.

 ```mbt check
 test {
   let left : Array[Char] = "abcd".iter().to_array()
   let right : Array[Char] = "bcde".iter().to_array()
   let matcher : @text.SequenceMatcher[Char] = @text.SequenceMatcher::new(
     left[:],
     right[:],
   )
   @test.assert_eq(matcher.ratio() > 0.7, true)
   let blocks = matcher.get_matching_blocks()
   @test.assert_eq(blocks.length(), 2)
 }
 ```

The suffixes of a text in sorted order, built by doubling: after round `k`
 every suffix is ranked by its first `2^k` units, and a round ranks the pairs
 of ranks it already has. Two rounds of comparison sort per doubling give
 O(n log^2 n), which is what a text of any ordinary size needs.

 ```mbt check
 test {
   let array : @text.SuffixArray = @text.SuffixArray::new("banana")
   @test.assert_eq(array.search("ana"), [1, 3])
   @test.assert_eq(array.length(), 6)
 }
 ```

A suffix automaton: every substring of the text is a path from the start
 state, and each state remembers how many times the substring it stands for
 occurs. The build is one pass and the state count is at most twice the text
 length, which is what makes "how often does this appear" a lookup.

 ```mbt check
 test {
   let automaton : @text.SuffixAutomaton = @text.SuffixAutomaton::new("banana")
   @test.assert_eq(automaton.contains("ana"), true)
   @test.assert_eq(automaton.count_occurrences("ana"), 2)
   @test.assert_eq(automaton.contains("band"), false)
 }
 ```

free_fns.doc:
The longest palindromic substring, as a half-open range. Manacher's scan
 keeps the palindrome around each centre, so the whole text costs one pass.

 ```mbt check
 test {
   @test.assert_eq(@text.longest_palindrome("forgeeksskeegfor"), (3, 13))
   @test.assert_eq(@text.longest_palindrome("abc"), (0, 1))
 }
 ```

The Z array of `text`: `z[i]` is the length of the longest run starting at
 `i` that is also a prefix of the text. It is the linear-time answer to "where
 does this prefix repeat", and it is what makes the suffix array's first
 ranking pass unnecessary.

 ```mbt check
 test {
   @test.assert_eq(@text.z_function("aabxaab"), [7, 1, 0, 0, 3, 1, 0])
 }
 ```

types.doc:
Builds a perfect hash table from the entries it is given. A key may appear
 once: the second `entry` for the same key is refused, because a table with
 two entries in one bucket would have no displacement that places both.

 ```mbt check
 test {
   let builder : @phf.PhfBuilder[String, Int32] = @phf.PhfBuilder::new()
   builder.entry("x", 1)
   let refused = try {
     builder.entry("x", 2)
     Ok(())
   } catch {
     @spec.Refused::DuplicateValue => Err(true)
     _ => Err(false)
   }
   assert_true(refused == Err(true))
 }
 ```

A set with the same layout as `PhfMap`, holding only the keys.

 ```mbt check
 test {
   let builder : @phf.PhfBuilder[String, Unit] = @phf.PhfBuilder::new()
   builder.entry("in", ())
   let set = builder.build_set()
   assert_true(set.contains("in"))
   assert_true(!set.contains("out"))
 }
 ```

The k-element subsets, in lexicographic order of positions.

 ```mbt check
 test {
   let combinations : @algo.Combinations[Int32] = @algo.Combinations::new(
     [1, 2, 3, 4],
     k=2,
   )
   @test.assert_eq(combinations.iter().to_array(), [
     [1, 2],
     [1, 3],
     [1, 4],
     [2, 3],
     [2, 4],
     [3, 4],
   ])
 }
 ```

The k-element selections that may repeat an element, in lexicographic order
 of positions.

 ```mbt check
 test {
   let with_repeats : @algo.CombinationsWithReplacement[Int32] = @algo.CombinationsWithReplacement::new(
     [1, 2],
     k=2,
   )
   @test.assert_eq(with_repeats.iter().to_array(), [[1, 1], [1, 2], [2, 2]])
 }
 ```

The reflected binary code over `bits` positions: consecutive words differ in
 exactly one bit, and the first is all zeroes.

 ```mbt check
 test {
   let code : @algo.GrayCode = @algo.GrayCode::new(2)
   @test.assert_eq(code.iter().take(4).to_array(), [
     [false, false],
     [false, true],
     [true, true],
     [true, false],
   ])
 }
 ```

The k-permutations of a run, in the order Python's `itertools.permutations`
 produces them: the positions advance like an odometer, so the first element
 changes slowest.

 ```mbt check
 test {
   let permutations : @algo.Permutations[Int32] = @algo.Permutations::new(
     [1, 2, 3],
     length=2,
   )
   @test.assert_eq(permutations.iter().to_array(), [
     [1, 2],
     [1, 3],
     [2, 1],
     [2, 3],
     [3, 1],
     [3, 2],
   ])
 }
 ```

Every subset, shortest first: the order
 `chain.from_iterable(combinations(s, r) for r in range(len(s) + 1))` gives.

 ```mbt check
 test {
   let powerset : @algo.Powerset[Int32] = @algo.Powerset::new([1, 2])
   @test.assert_eq(powerset.iter().to_array(), [[], [1], [2], [1, 2]])
 }
 ```

The cartesian product of several runs, rightmost pool varying fastest, the
 order `itertools.product` uses.

 ```mbt check
 test {
   let pools : Array[ArrayView[Int32]] = [[1, 2][:], [3, 4][:]]
   let product : @algo.Product[Int32] = @algo.Product::new(pools)
   @test.assert_eq(product.iter().to_array(), [[1, 3], [1, 4], [2, 3], [2, 4]])
 }
 ```
